[00:00] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Our current era of AI is one of exponential acceleration. Models are scaling into behemoths. Data centers are stacking endless rows of GPUs while burning ever'

[00:12] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'more power. AI labs are locked in a race for compute, energy, and capital. The sheer momentum of the whole industry is palpable. And yet, the story still feels incomplete. brute forcing today\'s'

[00:24] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'digital deterministic paradigm to unfathomable scales simply cannot be the endgame. For artificial intelligence to fulfill its full promise, we need a non-incremental game and the density of'

[00:36] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'intelligence. Our very existence, that of biological general intelligence, is proof that there exists a better way forward. One that is far removed from digital computation, one that harnesses'

[00:48] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the inherent randomness in nature and one that is far more parameter data and power efficient. At Extropic, we took guidance from nature to design such a new form of computing from the electrons up. By leaving conventional bits behind'

[01:01] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'and instead embracing pivots, we carved a new path that promises more intelligence per watt, per square millimeter, and per second. As we make our way along this path today, we have several updates to share with you which span our entire thermodynamic computing'

[01:14] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'stack. So join us on our journey to build the ultimate substrate for intelligence. Last fall, we announced X0, our first ever thermodynamic chip. With X0, we demonstrated novel'

[01:26] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'probabistic primitives in silicon that serve as our building blocks for our current and future chips. We also shipped our first desktop devices featuring this chip, the XTR0 experimental testing platform, which is'

[01:38] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'now in the hands of early adopters. Along with this test kit, we shipped our first software, Thermal, a library for thermodynamic hyperraphical models that lets developers experiment with thermopoming close to the metal. With'

[01:50] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'X0, we took thermodynamic computing from zero to one. Now, it\'s time to turn it up from one to 1 billion. Before we get to the hardware though, let us'

[02:04] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'start with some updates on our thermodynamic software stack. We heard you after our last launch. Cool alien computer, but how am I supposed to program this thing? So, we went out and built new layers of our stack inspired by the deep learning frameworks you know'

[02:17] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'and love to make thermodynamic programming feel less alien. Deep learning conquered software because one could write parameterized programs and find the best ones with brainy descent.'

[02:29] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Now, we\'re extending that same philosophy to the programming of stoastic devices. Today, we\'re excited to release our new open- source framework for general stoastic differentiable programming. Going beyond strictly thermodynamic programs, for the'

[02:42] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'first time, you can build stoastic programs and train them with gradient descent. Just like neural networks, you can apply them to simulations of nature, sampling, machine learning, and much more. In classical differentiable programming, you arrange modular'

[02:54] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'parameterized functions into a directed asyclic graph. Torx lets you build the same kind of graphs, but where the nodes are stocastic transition currents, their distributions over functions wired together in series in parallel. The'

[03:06] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'result is modular probabistic programs with depth in a natural flow, trainable end to end with your favorite optimizer. To put simply, Torx is to thermal with PyTorch is to CUDA. Torx is hardware agnostic and can run on stoastic'

[03:20] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'processing units of all kinds from our very own XTR0 today to our next chip Z1 and beyond. The white paper we\'re releasing today walks through a wide array of examples to get you started. Explore the docs, code, and examples at torqu.i.'

[03:33] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Alongside Torx, we\'re releasing a white paper on thermalizers, our upcoming thermodynamic compilation library. Thermalizers lets you compile arbitrary Torx programs onto thermodynamic devices'

[03:45] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'like Z1. It bridges Torx and thermal, thus completing our full thermodynamic programming stack. It works by variationally compiling each stochastic transition kernel in your program into a'

[03:58] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'hardware energy-based models over input and output variables. The result is a directed graph of thermal models that maps directly onto our thermodynamic hardware fabric, enabling stochastic program execution via in-memory pipeline'

[04:10] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'parallel sampling. Now, for any application, anyone can take a general highle stochastic program and readily map it onto thermodynamic hardware without having to manage low-level'

[04:22] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'device details. For the right workloads, this can deliver up to 10,000x greater energy efficiency than GPUs. You can read more about thermalizers in our recent blog post. You can go check out the white paper on archive and you can'

[04:34] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'watch our GitHub for the open source library release in the coming weeks. The generative AI era is one dominated by proistic software and people serious about proistic software should be'

[04:46] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'building it for probabistic hardware. At Extropic, we firmly believe in the power of codeesign as only what evolves together ultimately fits together. As such, our proistic software frameworks announced today were explicitly designed'

[04:59] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'with our next generation of proistic hardware in mind. And so today, we can finally share more about our next generation of hardware. At its core is our newest chip Z1. Z1 is comprised of over a quarter million pubits arranged'

[05:14] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'in a sparse graph. Each of these pits has 60 neighbor connectivity while supporting sampling rate of over 50 MHz. Z1 sits 269,000 pits on a chip that'

[05:26] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'consumes less than 1 watt while measuring less than 12 mm per side. We plan to make Z1 systems available in two form factors. First, an edge ready'

[05:38] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'thermomp compute stick with over a half million pubits in an M.2 form factor, enabling low power applications at the edge, such as next generation augmented reality and robotics. For the heaviest proistic workloads, we are also building'

[05:52] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Z1 accelerator cards. These thermodynamic accelerator cards pack over 4 million pits each, enabling scaled up thermodynamic workloads for the very first time. They come in'

[06:04] [scene:talk]
